.TXT 9 -40 222 0
Cg a69.375000,69.000000,1580
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {With the growth of computer usage
 in the past years, it is common for one
 computer to need to communicate with another.
  The advent and growth of the internet 
has caused  even greater need forcommunications
 between computers and computer systems.
  All of these applications use the telephone
 to provide the underlying communication
 path.  Using a raw telephone line is not
 suitable for sending computer signals, 
which generally represent a 1 as 5 volts
 and a 0 as 0 volts.  Two-level signals 
suffer considerable distortion when transmitted
 over a voice-grade telephone line, and 
this of course leads to bit errors.  We 
showed in chapter 5 that we could use AM
 to send the data, then in chapter 6 we 
found an even more efficient method in FM.
  Therefore, for our third project we chose
 the most popular method of data transmission
 FSK.  In frequency modulation the voltage
 level is constant but the carrier frequency
 is different for a 1or 0.  A person listening
 to FSK would hear two tones, corresponding
 to 1 and 0.  Because voice grade telephone
 provides only one channel for transmission
 the bits must be sent serially, one after
 the other ( or in groups of two if dibit
 is used). The device that accepts characters
 from a computer in the form of two-level
 signals, one bit at a time, and transmits
 the bits in groups of one or two in FSK
 is the }{\i0 \b modem}{.  Presently the
 baud rate for the modem is at 28,000 bps
 or one bit change every 35.5 }{\f1 m}{sec.
  Now that we have the motivation for our
 project let's implement it using the power
 of Mathcad.}}
}
.TXT 39 0 223 0
Cg a66.875000,69.000000,98
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
